guide.s2.body:
A case has two parts, just like the case form.

What all verified attorneys see, without your name:
• the practice area, that is what the case is about;
• the title and the brief;
• the states and city;
• the budget and urgency;
• the card cover;
• how many photos and files the case has and whether it has details, but not the photos, files or details themselves.

This part lets attorneys find your case and send a bid. So do not put a passport, SSN, account numbers, names, addresses or contacts in the title or the brief.

What only the attorney whose bid you accepted sees:
• the details: the full story, names of the parties, case number, addresses, dates;
• the case photos and files;
• your name and profile photo;
• your contacts, if the attorney has an active subscription.
This part opens only after you accept a bid. That attorney's assistants with access to cases or chats see it too.

Case comments are visible to everyone who sees the case, and you stay anonymous in them. Whether attorneys see that you are online depends on your privacy settings.

When you accept a bid, the other bids are declined automatically and those attorneys are notified.

If you blocked an attorney or they blocked you, they cannot see your case. A paused case leaves the attorneys' feed and search.
